{{ contract }}

### The harness assembles `{{ entry_file }}` and runs YOUR module like this — nothing else runs
```python
ctx.collection = <this zone's own Collection, active>   # bpy.ops primitives land in it
out = zones.<snake>.build(ctx)                           # the function MUST be named `build`, one argument
# a raise = this zone DROPPED (file:line reported), everything it made removed; the rest builds
```
So `{{ zone_file }}` defines `def build(ctx) -> bpy.types.Collection` that links everything into
`ctx.collection` and returns it.  `build_{{ zone_name }}`, `main` and `register` are never called.
Animation is DATA: keyframes on `ctx.frame(0.0)` / `ctx.frame(1.5)` and simple drivers of `frame`
(`lib/motion.py`); there is no per-frame hook.

## What this zone is graded on (the requirement, and how it is measured)
1. **What the description names is here.**  Counts the plan states are compared with the placement
   census (`missing_planned_element`, `empty_zone`), and a place that reads as sparse for what the brief
   describes is judged `undressed_scene`.  Place props where the setting puts them; regular arrangements
   only where the brief implies them.  `lib.scatter` offers `scatter`, `along_path`, `poisson`, `grid`
   if you want them.
2. **Repeated natural elements read as individuals** from the cameras (judge `monotonous_vegetation`:
   one shape stamped many times, in a ring or a grid).  The lib's `tree` kinds, `shrub`, `fern`,
   `boulder` seeds, `materials.tint` and per-instance rotation / scale are one way; regular things (a
   colonnade, a row of bollards) stay regular.
3. **Seated, and clear of each other — measured.**  Every placed root object rests on the surface it
   stands on (`lib.place.seat` / `rest_on`, or your own seating — the gate samples the foot columns):
   a foot 0.05 m up is `floating` (ERROR at 0.08 m), 0.10 m under is `sunken` (ERROR at 0.20 m), two
   boxes sharing > 10 % of the smaller one are `interpenetration` (ERROR at 35 %).  Run
   `lib.place.clear_of(objs)` over your roots AND the neighbours' placed things before you finish.
   `obj["placement"] = "free"` only for things that hang in the air; the share of tagged things is
   reported.  A built thing on soil must not read as pasted on (judge `flat_ground`: undressed seams):
   a few centimetres of sink (the gate allows up to 0.10 m) or dressing along the seam are options; a
   ground feature (a pond, a bed, a terrace) must not read as a plate on the terrain from the overview
   cameras — carving in `height_at` (the env's) or a built edge both work.
4. **The cameras stay clear.**  No authored camera may be inside or blocked by what this zone builds
   (`scene_views` camera_checks: nothing within 0.5 m of a lens, `camera_in_geometry`; 6 of 9 sight
   rays within 1.5 m, `camera_blocked` — each an ERROR capped at 0.50).  `lib.place.clear_mask` / a
   poisson `mask` keeps scatter out of any region you choose.  Framing is yours.
5. **Motion that shows.**  The harness DIFFERENCES the t=0 and t=1.5 frames per camera and reports the
   number: a camera moves when ≥ 0.4 % of its pixels change by more than 8/255 (or ≥ 0.08 % by more
   than 60/255); no authored camera moving is `no_motion` (ERROR), the judge's `nothing_moves`.
   Keyframes on `ctx.frame(0.0)` / `ctx.frame(1.5)` (LINEAR) or a simple driver of `frame`; what moves
   is the brief's (amplitudes that have registered at 1024 × 576: ≥ 0.10 rad, ≥ 0.05 m, a particle /
   leaf / boat ≥ 0.6 m per 1.5 s).
6. **One root = one thing; names unique.**  `check_placement` measures each root object in
   `ctx.collection` as one placed thing: a multi-part prop has one root (a mesh, a parent Empty, a
   collection instance) and every root a unique name (`unique_name(base, i)`).  Stay inside the triangle
   budget `scene_probe` reports; collection instances and geometry-nodes clouds
   (`lib.vegetation.grass_patch`, `lib.scatter.gn_scatter`) are the cheap ways to repeat, and the lint
   warns on `bpy.ops.mesh.primitive_*` inside a loop.
7. **A soft particle has no silhouette.**  In Cycles only a volume renders without an edge
   (`lib.materials.volume`); a translucent surface puff, metaball, torus or disc is graded
   `hard_edged_particles` and fails the brief's must-item.  It is one root tagged free, and it moves
   ≥ 0.6 m between the two frames.

## Before you return — what the argon scenes of iteration 0 lost their points to
Thirty argon Blender scenes were measured on 2026-10-15 (mean 0.75).  Every point below is one
tool call away and cost whole rounds:
1. **Your LAST `check_placement` of this session shows 0 ERROR rows for this zone.**  Every
   session ran the check; 13 of 30 picked rounds still shipped interpenetration ERRORs because the
   zone was edited again afterwards and never re-checked.  Edit → `build` → `check_placement` →
   fix EVERY row it names (move the smaller thing until `clear_of` reports no pair over the gate's
   10 %; lower / raise by the stated metres) → `build` → `check_placement` again until clean →
   only then `scene_views`.
2. **Scatter clear of what you placed**: 9 of the 13 were small props scattered INTO a cart, a
   rack, a shrub or a salt mound (100 % of the smaller box).  Place the big things first, keep every
   scatter point out of their footprints (`mask = clear_mask(placed_roots, margin=0.3)` from
   `lib.place` as the `poisson(..., mask=mask)` argument, or your own test), then
   `for a, b, frac in clear_of([*roots, *scattered]):` and move or delete the smaller offender
   (`bpy.data.objects.remove`) — the placement skill shows the loop.
3. **A pit, a bank or a floor you carve in this zone is the ground for what stands in it**: seat
   those things on YOUR surface (`rest_on(obj, pit_floor)`), not on `ctx.height_at`, or the gate
   reads the sheaves 0.4 m sunk and the anchor plates 0.28 m floating (as in one iteration-0 run).
4. **A soft particle has no silhouette**: in Cycles only a volume renders without an edge
   (`lib.materials.volume`; a cloud of `Batch` spheres wearing it is one root).  Translucent
   Principled puffs, metaballs, tori and discs were `hard_edged_particles` in 8 of 30 scenes AND
   failed the brief's "soft particle effect" must-item every time.  Tag the root
   `placement = "free"` and move it ≥ 0.6 m between the two frames.
5. **A cross-zone overlap is an `interpenetration` row like any other**: two runs shipped one (a
   wind pump into a salt mound, a lane prop into a canopy frame).  Run `clear_of` over your roots
   and the neighbours' placed things; the bbox is where the plan's contents are measured, not a
   fence.

